Z||000000||Gokart||04-10-2000||09:08 PM||||JINXED MYSELF! I've had enough "guessing games" wondering if it's the solenoid or the valve or a vaccum leak or a plug? I have no way of performing proper tests so I'm thinking about taking it to the other local dealer in my area. Only thing I'm remembering though.......are solenoids still available from GM? I guess I could have the anti-freeze smell checked as well. Any suggestions....this is getting old  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> When I replaced the plug that goes into the solenoid from my parts because of a broken prong, I just electrical taped the four wires together......should I have soldered them?<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 04-10-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||Old Lar||04-10-2000||10:24 PM||||My '87 is throwing out a 32 code also.  Since it is still snowing, I can't check out anything.  Mr Goodwrench has some other computers to hook up to the car.  Runs rough on idle when warm.  Stalls out if you turn on defroster (A/C goes on).  It goes in tomorrow for it's checkup.  Vacuum leak (?), EGR pipe (?), EGR selinoid (?)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000002||Fiasco||04-11-2000||04:11 AM||||I just had code 32 problems and it turned out to be the filter on the back of the vac. reg. solenoid. The breather line across to the air intake duct had come off and was sucking up all kinds of dust & crap. You can clean the filter out with compressed air into the solenoid end. -Mine was severely clogged but it's possible that if this vent line is cracked and leaking on yours, you could have the same problem. I would also invest in a MityVac hand-held vacuum pump. This allows you to trace vacuum lines and check for leaks. Also, the Haynes manual describes a good method of checking the EGR valve, the lines, and the solenoid with a hand-vacuum pump/gauge. Good luck.||||reg||3
Z||000003||mwbackus||04-11-2000||06:33 AM||||The ECM monitors the function of the EGR vacuum switch to determine correct operation of the EGR system.  If anything disrupts the operation of this switch such as a vacuum leak, clog, electrical short, or a defective switch, a code 32 will set.  Eliminate all of the easy and inexpensive varibles first, and if the problems still exists, replace the EGR solenoid.<P><BR>Mike B.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||04-11-2000||06:21 PM||||Just got my car back from Mr Goodwrench because of code 32, and rough idle. They used their diagnostic computer and found no component failure. They found two problems:  The timing was advanced and the throttle body was carboned up badly.  It sort of makes sense.  A new distributor was recently put in and I just got back from a 2700 mile trip to Florida and back.  The idle is now at about 800-900 rpm now and running smooth.  I'm beginning to think that code 32 is the most sensitive to being tripped and it pops up for a variety of reasons.  I'd drive it around a little more, but it's snowing now: 1-2 inches before morning.  I hope thats all.<P>Where's the spell check on this forum?<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 04-11-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000005||Gokart||04-11-2000||09:33 PM||||Well I ordered a solenoid from the Fiero Store this morning! Having 2nd thoughts about letting the dealer work on it. They said I could return it if not needed. Talked to the tech guys at the Fiero store and he said spliced wires at my plug could make a difference as well, will solder them this weekend. Maybe my timing is advanced too high as well? To retard I turn the distributer clockwise...right?||||reg||1
Z||000006||mwbackus||04-11-2000||10:47 PM||||Incorrect ignition timing will not set a code 32.  The only time a code 32 will set is if the ECM detects improper operation of the EGR vacuum switch.  Like I stated above, there are various possible causes of incorrect EGR switch operation, but nothing else will set this code.  A throttle body that is so crudded up that the EGR vacuum supply line port is clogged can definatly set a code 32 since the EGR vacuum switch will malfunction due to the improper vacuum flow.<P>Mike B.||||reg||1
